    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Low-back loading is debated. Sit-ups compress the lumbar spine above 3,000 N whether the knees are bent or straight (model estimates, McGill 1995 and 1998), and partial curl-ups give the best abdominal challenge for the least compression (Axler and McGill 1997); McGill recommends curl-ups over sit-ups. The counter-view (Contreras and Schoenfeld 2011; Ross, ACE 2011) is that the herniation data come from pig spines flexed thousands of times without tissue adaptation, and a 1,141-soldier trial found the same overall injury rates with sit-up training as with core stabilization (Childs 2010). The compression is well measured; whether it injures healthy trainees at normal volumes is not settled.",
        "Low-back pain, disc herniation or flexion intolerance: avoid, or swap for a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated or end-range spinal flexion, sit-ups included.",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): avoid sit-ups while rebuilding deep-core control.",
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Neck pain: let the hands support the head without pulling, use a towel under the head, or reduce the range.",
        "Feet lift before the 3/4 point: stop lower or regress; do not anchor the feet.",
        "Keep total flexion reps moderate (about 60 per session) and avoid the first hour after waking."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Crunch / McGill curl-up (head and shoulders only)",
        "Arms crossed on the chest",
        "Smaller range: stop at about 30-45 degrees"
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Full sit-up",
        "Hold a light plate on the chest",
        "Slower lowering (3-4 s)"
      ]
    }
